Psoriatic Arthritis Oligoarticular
Complex Fatigue Entities
Asymmetrical inflammatory arthritis

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: CFE-043
Reason for Visit: Urgent evaluation of severe, isolated joint swelling and extreme pain to rule out acute septic arthritis.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported sudden, massive swelling of the right index finger and left knee, accompanied by excruciating pain preventing movement or weight-bearing. Immediate physical assessment was mandated to rule out an acute, destructive bacterial joint infection (septic arthritis) before morning hours to prevent permanent joint damage.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: My finger looks like a hot sausage, my left knee is full of fluid, and the throbbing pain is keeping me awake.
History of Present Illness: Patient with known psoriasis reports an acute onset of severe asymmetrical joint pain evolving over 24 hours. The primary presentation involves classic dactylitis ("sausage digit") in the right index finger, rendering it completely immobile due to soft tissue swelling. Concurrently, the patient developed a large, painful joint effusion in the left knee. Patient notes severe nail pitting on the affected hand and significant systemic fatigue preceding the joint flare.
Functional Status: Ambulating with a severe limp; right hand is functionally useless for fine motor tasks.
Social/Safety Concerns: Living alone; unable to safely prepare food due to dominant hand involvement and knee pain.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 130/80 mmHg | HR: 88 bpm | Temp: 37.3 C | RR: 16 /min | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 75 Kg
General: Fatigued, nursing the right hand, avoiding weight-bearing on the left leg. Mild low-grade fever present.
Physical Exam: Right index finger exhibits profound, uniform soft-tissue swelling extending from the MCP to the DIP joint (dactylitis), distinctly violaceous and highly tender. Marked nail pitting observed. Left knee demonstrates a moderate, fluctuant effusion with warmth and significant restriction of terminal flexion. Cruciate and collateral ligaments are stable. No obvious skin portal of entry for infection near the affected joints. Findings represent classical asymmetrical inflammatory arthritis.
Environmental Observations: Safe environment. Ice packs noted on the coffee table.
Medication Reconciliation: No current DMARDs. Attempted Paracetamol with zero relief.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Psoriatic Arthritis Oligoarticular - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Acute Septic Arthritis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Asymmetrical involvement of two entirely distinct joint types (small digit and large weight-bearing joint) simultaneously, without high-grade spiking fever.

Gout - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Classical dactylitis (entire digit swelling) rather than focal monoarticular inflammation typical of podagra, and presence of prominent psoriatic nail changes.

Reactive Arthritis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of preceding gastrointestinal or genitourinary infection symptoms, and established psoriatic skin history.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient's acute presentation of severe, localized joint swelling and warmth mandated an urgent in-home evaluation to differentiate between an autoimmune flare and acute septic arthritis, which destroys cartilage rapidly. Upon confirming the classic clinical markers of oligoarticular PsA (dactylitis and asymmetrical large joint effusion without severe systemic toxicity), the risk of acute sepsis was clinically downgraded. Urgent administration of potent, prescription-level anti-inflammatories was required to halt the synovial and tenosynovial inflammation, restoring function and alleviating severe distress.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Prescribed a 5-day course of high-dose Celecoxib 200mg twice daily to aggressively target the dactylitis and knee effusion. Targeted intervention strategy includes LDN, COX-2 inhibitors, and Zinc. Advised relative rest of the affected joints.
Positive Behavioral Change: By continuously elevating your left knee and right hand above the level of your heart on pillows tonight, you will mechanically enlist gravity to drain the inflammatory fluid out of the joint spaces, directly reducing the capsular pressure and rewarding yourself with significantly less throbbing pain.
Home-Based Services Optional: None acutely.
DME/Safety Optional: Recommended walking stick for the right hand to offload the left knee.
Follow-up: Urgent GP review in 3 days for rheumatology referral and consideration of joint aspiration or local steroid injection.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate presentation to the Emergency Department via 000 if the knee becomes increasingly fiery red, tense, and hot to touch, or if a high fever with uncontrollable shivering develops.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: This asymmetrical presentation is highly characteristic of Psoriatic Arthritis, but any sudden worsening of a single joint must still be monitored for infection.
Clinical Course: The uniform swelling of the finger (dactylitis) is notoriously slow to resolve, often taking weeks of targeted therapy. The knee effusion should begin to soften within 48 hours of starting the NSAID.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Call 000 immediately if you develop uncontrollable shaking/shivering, a high fever, or if your knee suddenly becomes fiery red and unendurably hot.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Your immune system is attacking not just the joint itself, but the entire tendon sheath running down your finger, causing the "sausage" swelling; the high-dose medication is designed to specifically penetrate these thick tissues and stop the immune attack.
Actionable Steps: Do not attempt to forcefully bend or stretch the swollen finger tomorrow; resting the inflamed tendon completely prevents microscopic tearing of the swollen tissue, positively reinforcing a faster recovery of your hand function.

